[{:db/id 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c8480fc0-38a5-50e6-ac81-ff79f47d27a2" "question-3deca36d-f2e0-579a-9131-b3cacf95a814" "question-5a0569d9-4bfc-5b49-a516-f990725fdc02"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171794 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n\nII. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n\nIII. The weight of a child at birth" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171795 :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/71/714585d89ba3f1d529cd6b64658b778eafa8c346ff9ec592d4997e56803b913e.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda = 1/20$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda = 1/20,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{20} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/9f/9f4bcc34982b48061f0fda3c3136e97f37030cb1cf7d526919860ec53274c644.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}{e}^{-x/20}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/20}|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/4}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/4} \\\\  & ≈0.2212\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171796 :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/6a/6a82ddd5e5aa9cc641d3720adb9b998c9bc7e72c4e6edfb929d413a6fc584f87.svg)\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30$ purchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n![](images/79/7960b1171c6697fe49628a8f49ac07671d05acf323ebdfb9734175e12a605640.svg)\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 60) & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{60}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-x/30}|}_{60}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.1353.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171797 :question/worked-solution "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}$ requests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left( \\dfrac{1}{30} \\right).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30},$ $s=30,$ and $t=20.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30+20\\,|\\,X>30) & =P(X>20) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/30)\\cdot (20)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/3} \\\\  & ≈0.513.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8480fc0-38a5-50e6-ac81-ff79f47d27a2" :question/id #uuid "c8480fc0-38a5-50e6-ac81-ff79f47d27a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132463" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time between customer calls, in minutes, for a particular business\n\nII. The number of winning lottery tickets in a set of $10$ tickets\n\nIII. The number obtained when picking an integer number between $0$ and $15$ at random" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I can be modeled using an exponential distribution. Calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next call occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of winning lottery tickets is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number obtained is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8327a55a-2e34-5803-8492-faffa49567e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8327a55a-2e34-5803-8492-faffa49567e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "8327a55a-2e34-5803-8492-faffa49567e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9866233c-de4a-59cc-a272-02e41e15cca9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2923f92c-f047-52fe-9354-4e133888c105" "answer-00635c76-6a2d-5359-9c79-cf68e4a537ae" "answer-9866233c-de4a-59cc-a272-02e41e15cca9" "answer-d7b37614-ee7a-57bb-bc29-7f3a85ddcc73" "answer-4c961182-ebbc-558a-a08d-aab935e1f068"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2923f92c-f047-52fe-9354-4e133888c105" :answer/id #uuid "2923f92c-f047-52fe-9354-4e133888c105" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00635c76-6a2d-5359-9c79-cf68e4a537ae" :answer/id #uuid "00635c76-6a2d-5359-9c79-cf68e4a537ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9866233c-de4a-59cc-a272-02e41e15cca9" :answer/id #uuid "9866233c-de4a-59cc-a272-02e41e15cca9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7b37614-ee7a-57bb-bc29-7f3a85ddcc73" :answer/id #uuid "d7b37614-ee7a-57bb-bc29-7f3a85ddcc73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c961182-ebbc-558a-a08d-aab935e1f068" :answer/id #uuid "4c961182-ebbc-558a-a08d-aab935e1f068" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3deca36d-f2e0-579a-9131-b3cacf95a814" :question/id #uuid "3deca36d-f2e0-579a-9131-b3cacf95a814" :question/math-academy-id "q-132448" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time until a laser fails, in hours, in a specific type of machine\n\nII. The number obtained when launching a fair roulette wheel\n\nIII. The number of children in a daycare center" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Laser failure in a particular machine can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the time it takes for the laser to fail.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. Moreover, the number obtained when launching a roulette wheel is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of children is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a92619f-b432-58af-81b2-c7de5e2f81be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a92619f-b432-58af-81b2-c7de5e2f81be" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a92619f-b432-58af-81b2-c7de5e2f81be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a468f832-3368-5771-bc32-4531a9f82886" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1916b91d-3dd2-5b7d-8065-6c9e62cb7944" "answer-67e34ad7-4389-5131-b10c-f7c33576ead6" "answer-a468f832-3368-5771-bc32-4531a9f82886" "answer-48c12e76-6dfc-5fcc-8a40-a983537294e7" "answer-4a18a4a3-0167-535b-9b6b-71a22c559836"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1916b91d-3dd2-5b7d-8065-6c9e62cb7944" :answer/id #uuid "1916b91d-3dd2-5b7d-8065-6c9e62cb7944" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67e34ad7-4389-5131-b10c-f7c33576ead6" :answer/id #uuid "67e34ad7-4389-5131-b10c-f7c33576ead6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a468f832-3368-5771-bc32-4531a9f82886" :answer/id #uuid "a468f832-3368-5771-bc32-4531a9f82886" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48c12e76-6dfc-5fcc-8a40-a983537294e7" :answer/id #uuid "48c12e76-6dfc-5fcc-8a40-a983537294e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a18a4a3-0167-535b-9b6b-71a22c559836" :answer/id #uuid "4a18a4a3-0167-535b-9b6b-71a22c559836" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5a0569d9-4bfc-5b49-a516-f990725fdc02" :question/id #uuid "5a0569d9-4bfc-5b49-a516-f990725fdc02" :question/math-academy-id "q-96681" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time it takes for a taxi to reach any address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nII. The time it takes for a taxi to reach a particular address in a city, starting from the same point\n\nIII. The number of defective electronic components produced in a factory every week" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow an exponential distribution. The times needed to reach different destinations will likely have different average rates.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Journeys along a particular route can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time needed to reach the destination.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective components is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-24bbf2f1-e6cf-53b2-8967-353915ff3d65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-24bbf2f1-e6cf-53b2-8967-353915ff3d65" :answer-field/id #uuid "24bbf2f1-e6cf-53b2-8967-353915ff3d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6bac048-db5d-5859-b7b9-077b9d9c962c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-112ab786-9dc2-56b1-ad95-98851cd1d5fc" "answer-154c2087-4f7e-5899-993c-d47ac1fb33e2" "answer-bf065d18-e87f-5a12-9d23-987422ac4b16" "answer-8cfc39d2-61a4-56e6-90bf-ad85f0cc4e4c" "answer-c6bac048-db5d-5859-b7b9-077b9d9c962c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-112ab786-9dc2-56b1-ad95-98851cd1d5fc" :answer/id #uuid "112ab786-9dc2-56b1-ad95-98851cd1d5fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-154c2087-4f7e-5899-993c-d47ac1fb33e2" :answer/id #uuid "154c2087-4f7e-5899-993c-d47ac1fb33e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf065d18-e87f-5a12-9d23-987422ac4b16" :answer/id #uuid "bf065d18-e87f-5a12-9d23-987422ac4b16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cfc39d2-61a4-56e6-90bf-ad85f0cc4e4c" :answer/id #uuid "8cfc39d2-61a4-56e6-90bf-ad85f0cc4e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6bac048-db5d-5859-b7b9-077b9d9c962c" :answer/id #uuid "c6bac048-db5d-5859-b7b9-077b9d9c962c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172642 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads\n\nII. The number of incidents in a month at a particular crossroads\n\nIII. The speed of a car that arrives at a particular crossroads" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Incidents can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next incident occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of incidents in a month is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171477 :answer-field/correct 17592186171479 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173121 :tutorial/content "A **Poisson process** is a mathematical model used to describe events occurring randomly over continuous space or time. A Poisson process is characterized by the following properties:\n\n- Events occur *independently*. In other words, one event's occurrence does not affect another event's likelihood.\n\n- Events occur at a *constant average rate*. The rate at which events occur represents the expected number of events per unit of time or space.\n\nWe encountered the exponential distribution in a previous lesson. The exponential distribution models the amount of continuous space or time $X$ between two events in a Poisson process with success rate $\\lambda.$ \n\nWe have already seen that if $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ follows an exponential distribution, then the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/72/72cd99ec8ff51659357f00417203ace84164027e0cb88584cc136f608d86b15a.svg)\n\nThe exponential distribution is often considered the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. Recall that the geometric distribution models the number of trials before the first success in a sequence of independent Bernoulli trials.\n\nFor example, suppose a call center receives telephone calls at an average rate of $3$ calls per minute. If the random variable $X$ represents the amount of time between two consecutive calls, then $X$ can be modeled using an exponential distribution, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the time between two consecutive calls is less than one minute is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-3x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-3}]+[1] \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈0.9502.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. If the call center receives calls at an average rate of $3$ per minute, then the probability of waiting less than one minute until the next call is received should be relatively large." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173122 :tutorial/content "The memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that the probability of an event occurring in the future is independent of the time already passed. \n\nFor a random variable $X$, the probability that the event occurs in the next $t$ units of time, given it hasn't occurred after $s$ units of elapsed time, is\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSuppose you have a radioactive particle whose decay time $X$ follows the exponential distribution with rate $\\lambda = 0.8 \\text{ decays/minute}.$\n\nThen, the probability that the particle will decay in the first minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0.8{e}^{-0.8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-0.8x}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-0.8}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.5507.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose the particle doesn't decay in the first minute. What is the probability that it will decay in the next minute? We can answer this question using the memoryless property.\n\nBy the memoryless property, we have\n\n$$\nP(X > s + t \\mid X > s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complement of this event, we have\n\n$$\nP(X < s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X < t) = 1-e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $s=1$ and $t=1,$ so\n\n$$\nP(X < 1+1 \\, \\big|\\, X>1) = P(X < 1) \\approx 0.5507\n$$\n\nIn other words, even if the particle has survived for one minute, the probability that it will decay in the next minute remains the same as at the start of the observations." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172175 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
